{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And in all the mighty hand.</b> This is the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, about which it is written (Exodus 14:31): ״וירא ישראל את היד הגדולה״." "<b>And in all the great terror.</b> This is the standing at Mount Sinai, about which it is written (Exodus 20): ״ובעבור תהיה יראתו על פניכם לבלתי תחטאו״. And thus the signs and wonders were in order: these for Pharaoh first, and these for Israel afterward." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״ולכל היד החזקה ולכל המורא הגדול אשר עשה משה״ — the Tiferet and the Kavod. And this is what the prophet said (Isaiah 8): ״והוא מוראכם והוא מעריצכם״. And the expression ״אשר עשה״ is as if it said 'אשר הראה' — 'that he showed.' And the reason it did not say ״ולכל המורא הגדול״ and ״ולכל היד החזקה״ in the order of the verse that says ״הוא מוראכם והוא מעריצכם״ — all is for the preeminence of the prophecy of Moses, who was bonded in the מאירה (the illuminating speculum). And Scripture tells you that Moses our master, peace be upon him, showed and made understood the attributes before the eyes of all Israel. And its parallel, in my opinion, is (Deuteronomy 33): ״צדקת ה' עשה ומשפטיו עם ישראל״ — it says that Moses our master, peace be upon him, performed righteousness and justice with Israel." "<b>And you need</b> to know that because the Torah and the thought of Israel preceded the world, as we have received from our sages, knowers of hidden things, discerners of all that is concealed, therefore the Torah began with the word בראשית and is sealed with the word ישראל. And this Torah which Moses set before the children of Israel — Torah of truth, eternal salvation, for our good all the days — began with the letter ב׳ and is concluded with the letter למ״ד, for it is drawn from the supernal Wisdom which in the word ב׳ of בראשית is grasped in the two and thirty paths of Wisdom. And when inverted, like a seal reversing its arrangement, there is in this an allusion that all things shall return to their origin and their root. Therefore the matter of concluding the Torah with this supreme letter was to allude to the root of the supernal Tree, upon whose existence all beings are witnesses — from Him is all and unto Him is the end of all. And to this the Psalmist alluded with wondrous providence, when he begins with למ״ד (Psalms 24:1): ״לה' הארץ ומלואה״ — for from there, to those who know understanding, every action is first revealed, and there is its end and there is its return on high." "<b>And blessed be</b> the Rock of my life, hidden from the eye, revealed in my imagination, who has not forsaken His lovingkindness and His truth from His servant, who helped me and taught me and established me in the horn of light, and granted me merit to open and to close in the elucidation of the Torah, in which I intended His great and awesome Name. May He grant me merit for the day of good tidings, renew our days as of old and as in days gone by, and may the House and the Hall be built, and there with song let us raise a shout for Him, when we visit His sanctuary; there let us gaze from the radiance of the brilliance of the Tiferet and the Kavod, and in His hall all of it declares Kavod. There let us come to the altar of Elohim, and let us praise and say: Blessed be HaShem, the Elohim, the Elohim of Israel, and blessed be the Name of His Kavod forever, and may His Kavod fill all the earth, Amen and Amen. <br><b>It is finished and completed.</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:53.358641325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}